WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0018: two hourglasses,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.067
Two parts: an example by hand,
then code that finds a plan for any goal.

3
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:15.033
Two hourglasses: A measures 7 minutes,
and B measures 11. At time 0 all the sand is down.

4
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:20.133
You may turn a glass over at any moment, even midway:
what was down goes up, and keeps falling.

5
00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:23.667
At exactly time D
some hourglass must run out.

6
00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:29.633
Part A: glasses of 7 and 11 minutes.
Measure exactly 15 minutes from time 0.

7
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:34.567
The two glasses, all the sand down,
and a clock showing how much time has passed.

8
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:39.533
There is no other clock: only a glass running out
tells us how much time has passed.

9
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:43.633
The idea: 15 = 11 + 4,
and the 4 comes from the difference 11 − 7.

10
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:48.367
Time 0: turn both glasses over,
and both start running.

11
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:52.600
After 7 minutes A runs out.
B has 4 minutes left on top.

12
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.567
Time 7: turn A over at once,
and it starts another 7 minutes.

13
00:00:56.567 --> 00:01:01.233
At time 11 B runs out. Meanwhile A has dropped
4 minutes, with 3 left on top.

14
00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:05.333
The 4 minutes at the bottom of A are exactly
the difference 11 − 7 we were looking for.

15
00:01:05.633 --> 00:01:10.300
Time 11: turn A over midway,
and the 4 at the bottom go up.

16
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.267
A runs out at 11 + 4 = 15:
we measured exactly 15 minutes.

17
00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:18.267
The plan: both at 0, A at 7,
and A again at 11. Turning midway is the key.

18
00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:23.600
This is the page's box:
the time line, the clock and the two glasses.

19
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.733
Event 1, time 0: turn both over.

20
00:01:26.733 --> 00:01:30.033
Event 2, time 7: A runs out,
and we turn it over.

21
00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:34.700
Event 3, time 11: B runs out. A has
3 on top and 4 below, and we turn it over.

22
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.167
Event 4, time 15: A runs out.
We measured 15 minutes.

23
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:44.367
Not every D can be measured from 0. With 7 and 11
only 7, 11, 14, 15, and every number from 17.

24
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:49.167
Not 4 either: the difference 11 − 7 is a measure
that starts at time 7, not at time 0.

25
00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:55.200
A trap: turning over only a glass that ran out.
Then you only measure sums of whole runs.

26
00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.167
15 is not 7i + 11j for any i and j,
so without turning midway there is no solution.

27
00:02:00.966 --> 00:02:06.066
Part B: a function that takes A, B and D
and returns a plan, or that there is none.

28
00:02:06.766 --> 00:02:12.166
There is no other clock, so we decide only when
a glass runs out. Such a moment is an event.

29
00:02:12.166 --> 00:02:16.399
The state at an event: the time t,
and the sand on top of each glass, a and b.

30
00:02:17.199 --> 00:02:22.366
Four options from each state: turn nothing,
turn A, turn B, or turn both.

31
00:02:22.366 --> 00:02:27.599
Turning leaves A − a on top. We wait
for the first glass to run out: the smallest of them.

32
00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:31.099
And we reach the next state, at the next event.

33
00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:35.566
We drop an option where no glass runs,
and an option that goes past D.

34
00:02:36.366 --> 00:02:41.099
For example, (7, 0, 4) leads to four states.
Three of them were found before.

35
00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:44.633
Only (11, 3, 0) is new, and it is the one
that leads to the solution.

36
00:02:44.633 --> 00:02:48.633
BFS goes through the states in order,
and a state already seen does not enter the queue again.

37
00:02:49.433 --> 00:02:52.433
Now let's write it in Python.

38
00:02:54.466 --> 00:02:58.433
deque is a queue: we add at the end,
and take from the front.

39
00:03:00.033 --> 00:03:03.499
solve takes the two glasses
and the goal D.

40
00:03:08.233 --> 00:03:12.733
The start (0, 0, 0) is queued with an empty plan,
and it is also in the set seen.

41
00:03:16.266 --> 00:03:20.766
While the queue is not empty, take a state
and the plan that led to it.

42
00:03:22.799 --> 00:03:26.533
If its time is exactly D,
its plan is the answer.

43
00:03:30.866 --> 00:03:34.966
Two loops: turn A or not,
and turn B or not.

44
00:03:38.999 --> 00:03:42.966
Turning leaves A − a on top.
Without turning the amount stays.

45
00:03:49.199 --> 00:03:53.699
The glasses still running. If there are none,
nothing will ever happen: continue.

46
00:03:56.099 --> 00:04:00.566
Wait for the first glass to run out:
the smallest of the positive amounts.

47
00:04:03.533 --> 00:04:06.033
If that goes past D, drop it.

48
00:04:10.933 --> 00:04:15.233
The next state: the time grows by wait,
and the sand on top drops by wait.

49
00:04:19.499 --> 00:04:23.166
A state already seen does not enter again.
A new one goes into seen.

50
00:04:31.033 --> 00:04:35.533
The action: what we turned at time t.
The state enters the queue with the longer plan.

51
00:04:37.366 --> 00:04:40.899
If the queue is empty, there is no solution:
return None.

52
00:04:41.599 --> 00:04:46.766
Let's run it on 7, 11, 15. The variables under the code,
and on the right every state taken from the queue.

53
00:04:46.766 --> 00:04:50.966
(0, 0, 0) is in the queue and in seen,
with an empty plan.

54
00:04:50.966 --> 00:04:55.999
Take (0, 0, 0). The time is 0, not 15,
so go over the four options.

55
00:04:55.999 --> 00:05:01.033
Turn nothing: no sand on top, no glass
runs, so continue.

56
00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:05.633
Turn B: wait 11 minutes.
(11, 0, 0) is new and enters the queue.

57
00:05:05.633 --> 00:05:10.133
A alone gives (7, 0, 0), and both give
(7, 0, 4). Three states in the queue.

58
00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:14.933
Take (11, 0, 0). Every option
goes past 15, and nothing enters.

59
00:05:14.933 --> 00:05:18.833
(7, 0, 0) adds (14, 0, 0)
and (14, 0, 4).

60
00:05:18.833 --> 00:05:23.133
(7, 0, 4): only turning A
gives a new state, (11, 3, 0).

61
00:05:23.133 --> 00:05:27.033
(14, 0, 0) and (14, 0, 4):
every continuation goes past 15.

62
00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:31.766
(11, 3, 0) adds (14, 0, 8),
(15, 0, 0) and (15, 0, 7).

63
00:05:31.766 --> 00:05:34.766
(14, 0, 8) adds nothing.

64
00:05:34.766 --> 00:05:39.966
Take (15, 0, 0): t = D,
and return its plan.

65
00:05:39.966 --> 00:05:43.966
The plan returned: both at 0, A at 7,
and A again at 11. Part A's plan.

66
00:05:44.766 --> 00:05:49.799
In the page's box, the same search pop
after pop: the children, the queue and the tree.

67
00:05:49.799 --> 00:05:54.533
The first pop: three new states
in the queue, and the tree starts to grow.

68
00:05:54.533 --> 00:05:59.433
The fourth pop, (7, 0, 4):
only (11, 3, 0) is new.

69
00:05:59.433 --> 00:06:04.333
The seventh pop, (11, 3, 0),
puts (15, 0, 0) into the queue.

70
00:06:04.333 --> 00:06:08.799
At the ninth pop (15, 0, 0) comes out,
and the path to it is marked in the tree.

71
00:06:09.599 --> 00:06:14.999
At every event at least one glass is empty, so
there are at most (D+1)(A+B+1) states.

72
00:06:14.999 --> 00:06:18.999
Each state enters the queue once, with four
checks. In the example only 9 states were taken.

73
00:06:19.799 --> 00:06:24.899
A trap: dropping seen. The search
still ends, since the time grows every step.

74
00:06:24.899 --> 00:06:29.133
But at D = 60 it takes 5,403 states,
instead of 244 with seen.

75
00:06:29.927 --> 00:06:35.527
In short: turning midway gives 4 minutes,
and BFS over (t, a, b) finds a plan for any D.

76
00:06:35.527 --> 00:06:40.927
Try your own glasses and goals in the box
on the page of question 0018 on the site.
